  "preview": "Diego is building a kite and needs more than 2 5/8 meters of ribbon. The clearance bin has six tangled remnants. Which ones are long enough?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Convert between fractions and decimals (terminating and repeating), compare rational numbers with mixed representations, and place values on a number line to justify decisions.",
    "studentPrompt": "Diego is at a craft store building a stunt kite. He needs more than 2 5/8 meters of ribbon for the tail. The clearance bin has six remnants left:\n\n- Remnant A: 2.6 m\n- Remnant B: 2 3/4 m\n- Remnant C: 2.625 m\n- Remnant D: 2 11/16 m\n- Remnant E: 2 2/3 m\n- Remnant F: 2.58 m\n\n1. Convert every length to a decimal. If a decimal does not terminate, round to the nearest hundredth.\n2. Draw a number line from 2.5 to 2.8. Place all six remnants AND the threshold (2 5/8 m) on it.\n3. Sort the remnants into two groups: long enough for Diego's kite, and not long enough. Defend the borderline cases.\n4. Diego decides to also tie a 1/4 m bow at the bottom of the tail. Which remnants are long enough now?",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/31280807/pexels-photo-31280807.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "A vibrant collection of decorative ribbons neatly organized in a wooden display.",
      "credit": "Photo by Christina & Peter on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Hold up a measuring tape pulled to 2 5/8 m. Tell the story: 'Diego needs MORE THAN this much.' Show a slip of paper that reads '2.625 m'. Ask: 'Is this long enough? How do you know?' Once they wrestle with that single comparison, send them to the boards with the full list.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Ruler or meter stick (optional)",
      "Calculator (optional, for repeating decimals)"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "fraction-decimal-conversion",
      "comparing-rational-numbers",
      "number-line",
      "repeating-decimals",
      "real-world-application"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC fit. The low floor is 'pick one remnant and convert it.' The diamond is the borderline cases — Remnant A (2.6) and Remnant F (2.58) sit right around the 2.625 threshold and force students to be precise about hundredths. Repeating decimal E (2.666...) rewards careful rounding.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Most groups convert everything to decimals first. Some convert the threshold 2 5/8 = 2.625 and use it as a benchmark.\n- Strong groups notice that 2 11/16 = 2.6875 and 2 3/4 = 2.75 are clearly above; 2.58 is clearly below — they focus debate on Remnant A.\n- For Q3, the sharp question is Remnant A: 2.6 vs. 2.625. Some groups round 2.625 down to 2.6 and mis-tie them. Strong groups insist 'MORE THAN 2.625' rules A out.\n- For Q4, the new threshold is 2 5/8 + 1/4 = 2 7/8 = 2.875. ONLY remnants above 2.875 work — and looking at the list, none of them do. That's the surprise.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Treating 2 5/8 as 2.58 (confusing fraction notation with decimal).\n- Rounding 2.666... to 2.67 and then claiming it equals 2 2/3 exactly — it's a rounded value.\n- Saying 2.6 is 'the same as' 2.60 is 'the same as' 2.625 because all start with 2.6.\n- For Q4: adding 1/4 to each REMNANT instead of to the threshold.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Which remnant was hardest to decide on? What pushed you one way or the other?\n- Remnant E is 2 2/3 m. As a decimal it doesn't end. How did your group handle that?\n- In Q4, what changed when Diego added the bow? Why did that change the threshold and not the remnants?",
    "extensions": "- Diego's friend Mira needs LESS THAN 2 5/8 m for a smaller kite. Re-sort the same remnants for her. Compare the two sortings — do any remnants work for BOTH kids? Explain.\n- The store sells ribbon at $1.40 per meter (rounded to the nearest cent). What does each qualifying remnant cost? Which is the best deal per meter? (Hint: trick question — they're all the same rate. Why is the *total* still different?)\n- Build your own remnant list: invent six lengths (using a mix of fractions, terminating decimals, and repeating decimals) where exactly three are above 2 5/8 and three are below. Trade with another group.",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. A: 2.60, B: 2.75, C: 2.625, D: 2.6875 (round 2.69), E: 2.666... (round 2.67), F: 2.58. Threshold: 2 5/8 = 2.625.\n2. Number line 2.5 to 2.8, ticks every 0.05. Order: F (2.58), A (2.60), C (2.625 = threshold), E (~2.67), D (~2.69), B (2.75).\n3. Long enough (strictly greater than 2.625): E, D, B. Not long enough: F, A, C. Borderline: A is 2.60, which is BELOW 2.625 — out. C equals 2.625 exactly — NOT 'more than,' so also out.\n4. New threshold: 2 5/8 + 1/4 = 2 5/8 + 2/8 = 2 7/8 = 2.875. None of the remnants exceed 2.875 — Diego cannot use any of them for the kite + bow. He needs a longer remnant.",
